In article <1992Aug18.021933.1@cc.curtin.edu.au>, zrepachol@cc.curtin.edu.au
 (Paul Repacholi) writes...
>The last 11 processor was the J-11 in 1983. There is a lot of noise about a
>different board/box ever so often.

	Agreed, the J-11 has not changed since '83, but there would be no
	earthly reason to add new instructions, al la the 86/286/386/xxx.
	
	Upping chip speed also does not bring great speed increases if one
	is limited by Unibus or Q-bus speeds. However the current 11/93 and
	11/94 systems do attack the other limitations in performance, speci-
	fically with memory on CPU board, including a fast CPU to memory inter-
	connect, (PMI,) which gets around the limitation of the UNIBUS or QBUS
	on memory access.

	As a result, current top PDP11's are about equivalent to a VAX 11/780.
	For most of the mature applications PDP11's are used for, (a very di-
	verse lot, when I get my truck's emission checked, a PDP11 under RSX
	is running the show,) blinding CPU performance is not required.

	Most notably, the current DEC-direct catalog even lists CPU accelerator
	boards for the venerable PDP-11/70, a CPU approaching 15 years of age.

	Try finding accelerator boards for 11/780's in DEC-direct.

