In article <1992Aug11.212818.19498@thijssen.nl>, lamhof@thijssen.nl (Lambert Hofstra) writes:
>With the current VAX processor speeds (30 VUP) and the coming Alpha
>systems, it's not to be expected that DEC will develop much faster VAX
>processors. The current VAXstations are still slower than the new
>systems from SUN, IBM and HP. SUN now tries to increase the processing
>power of their systems by adding more processors. This is what DEC did a
>few years ago with the VAXstation 3520 and 3540, two and four processor
>VAXstations build with 3VUP (i think) processors. The VAXstation 3540
>performed quite well. This brings me to my first question:

Processor speed has been climbing real fast lately in the VAX product line.
In addition, in most instances the VAX processors despite being a CISC 
machine come quite close in performance to RISC boxes on the market from
Sun, IBM, and HP.  Also, if you look in the trade magazines, a lot of the
new VAXes are faster than the RISC boxes in transactions per second with
a lower price tag.  IMHO, the VAX/VMS systems are a little pricey, but 
they are more robust and resistent to failures and security problems than other 
systems.  In addition, the VAX/VMS systems provide a tremndous full-function
programming environment.  I have been working with VAX/VMS for the past 8
years, and I still have more to learn.  I should also note that price tags
for new VAX systems have also been coming down.  For instance, I think you
can pick up a VAXstation 4000/VLC for about $3500.  That's not bad at all!

I also believe that DEC is trying to keep production costs down in their 
current VAXstation line.  I really doubt that you will see multi-processor
VAXstations, but I believe that the new VAXstations are quite fast.  I 
do not believe they really need much more speed to compete effectively.
One thing DEC could work on is, DECwindows/Motif runs incredibly slow.
I have VAXstation II/GPX (16MB, DECwindows) that can pop up windows faster 
than a VAXstation 4000/60 (40MB, DECwindows/Motif).

Also, I believe that you will see new VAX processors still coming out even after
the Alpha computer systems start shipping.  Just look at the PDP-11 computer
line.  They are still making the darn things.  Every once in a while you see
a new faster PDP-11 processor being released.  I believe the release of new
VAX processors may decline over time, but I doubt they will stop.  There are
probably too many products that really require a real VAX.  Anyone using ELN
on an embedded VAX processor will probably require a real VAX processor for
instance.

Walter H. Dick, III
WHD0675@RITVAX.ISC.RIT.EDU


