MMODINFO:     THE  MACROMODEL   INFORMATION  MAIL  EXPLODER  WITH
ASSOCIATED ANONYMOUS  FTP ARCHIVES.     PLEASE READ THE FOLLOWING
CAREFULLY.

I.   WHAT IS THE MMODINFO LIST?

     The  MMODINFO  LIST  is  an  e-mail  exploder  which  allows
MacroModel  users  and  computational  chemistry  researchers  to
exchange information  and experience.    The  list  and  archives
reside on  a VAX  6420 running  the VMS  operating system  at the
University of  Toledo.  This list is not restricted and anyone is
welcome to  subscribe.   It works on the principle, "WHAT ANYBODY
POSTS, EVERYBODY  GETS." All  postings to  this list are archived
and available  for downloading  as described  below.  The list of
subscribers, however, will not be made available.

     Examples of topics for the list are:

     reports on  bugs in  the MacroModel  software or  associated
     data files and possible work-arounds.

     announcements of  new  versions  of  MacroModel  or  related
     software.

     comparison of MacroModel or results obtained with MacroModel
     to other programs and results.

     announcements of  MacroModel-related workshops  and symposia
     on new methods and techniques in computational chemistry.

     questions and answers about solving MacroModel problems.

     parameters, programs  and utilities  -  information  on  the
     availability of particular software, data, etc.

     hardware-related issues relevant to the MacroModel community
     opinions about services and products.

     Examples of topics which SHOULD NOT appear on this list are:

     personal attacks.
     topics unrelated to MacroModel.
     announcements of "vaporware".
     address inquires (you can post address inquire only when you
     exhausted all  other ways (i.e., looking up ACS Directory of
     Chemistry  Departments  in  your  library,  contacting  list
     coordinator,  contacting   the  Postmaster   at  the  target
     machine).

II.  HOW TO SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE.

     To subscribe  to the  MMODINFO List,  send e-mail containing
     the message 

	 SUBSCRIBE MMODINFO   
     to
	 MMODINFO-REQUEST@UOFT02.UTOLEDO.EDU.

     To  unsubscribe,  send  an  e-mail  containing  the  message

	 UNSUBSCRIBE MMODINFO   
     to
	 MMODINFO-REQUEST@UOFT02.UTOLEDO.EDU.

     Please, DO NOT SEND THESE MESSAGES TO EVERYBODY.

III. HOW TO POST TO THE MMODINFO LIST.

     The list  is uncensored,  i.e. nobody  screens the  messages
before they are posted.  The machine delivers them automatically.
Before you post to the list, be sure that your message is correct
(use an  editor!) and  adheres to  the rules.   Use lines shorter
than 80  characters since  many mailers  truncate  longer  lines.
Remember that  what you  post will  (we hope) be received by many
people in  many countries.  However, do not feel overly paralyzed
about this.   Communication  is a  two-way street.    Be  concise
though.  Not everybody has superworkstations connected to a high-
speed network.  However, do not be overly concise.  If you have a
question to  the list, describe your problem, and why you need it
--- try  to educate and be educated.  Also, summarize to the list
the responses  you got.  We want to know what you know.  The most
important part of your posting is the "Subject:" line.  It should
not be  longer than 40 characters.  People screen incoming e-mail
by the  "Subject:" line  and are  annoyed if  this  line  is  not
informative.   If you are posting a long file (e.g.  sample input
or output), please split it into chunks which are smaller than 50
kBytes.  Always identify part number and total number of parts in
the "Subject:"  line (e.g.  Part 2  of 5).  To post an article to
the   the    list   just    send   your    electronic   mail   to
MMODINFO@UOFT02.UTOLEDO.EDU or MMODINFO@UOFT02.BITNET the way you
normally do.    It  will  be  automatically  distributed  to  all
subscribers.   However, remember that you might not get a copy of
your own  post since many mailers suppress messages with the same
From: and  To: lines.  To ensure getting your own post place your
own e-mail address on the Cc: line or ask your system manager for
help (depending  on the  system, some  e-mail defaults  needs  be
changed).   If you  are not sure that your post is successful, or
if  you   have  problems   or  suggestions,   contact  the   list
administrator, Douglas A. Smith, at DSMITH@UOFT02.UTOLEDO.EDU.  I
am here to help you.

IV.  SOFTWARE ANNOUNCEMENTS.

     While the  main  software  of  interest  for  this  list  is
MacroModel, it  is recognized that many MacroModel users also use
other computational  chemistry  software,  including  commercial,
public domain  and home grown.  Persons wishing to announce their
own MacroModel-related  software, whether  it be freely available
or available  for a  fee, may  do so  on this list.  Such persons
should also  be willing  to respond to user questions, as well as
bug  reports,  requests  for  comments,  and  tips  on  efficient
software use.

V.   EMPLOYMENT (POSITIONS OFFERED AND WANTED)

     To limit  traffic on the list to matters of general interest
the announcements  of positions wanted and positions offered will
be  handled   off  line.     Instead  of  sending  such  mail  to
MMODINFO@UOFT02.UTOLEDO.EDU     send     them     directly     to
DSMITH@UOFT02.UTOLEDO.EDU  or   DSMITH@UOFT02.BITNET.     I  will
collect   these   announcements   and   archive   them   in   the
CHEMISTRY.POSITIONS directory  available  through  anonymous  ftp
(see below).   I  will try to post a short summary of these files
from time  to time.   Please  include the description of position
offered/wanted, degree  needed/acquired, salary, contact address,
etc.   The announcements  can be  any length,  but remember  that
people do  not read lengthly essays.  When the offer is no longer
current, please  send me a request to remove it from the archive.
All announcements  will be  deleted 10  weeks after their initial
posting regardless.

VI.  ARCHIVES OF THE MMODINFO LIST.

     Currently, archives are accessible only via anonymous ftp at
     FTP.UTOLEDO.EDU (131.183.1.1)

     all mail sent to the list (in [.MMOD]).
     software and  utility programs submitted by list subscribers
     (in [.CHEMISTRY.SOFTWARE]).
     positions         offerred         announcements         (in
     [.CHEMISTRY.POSITIONS.OFFERRED]).
     positions         wanted          announcements          (in
     [.CHEMISTRY.POSITIONS.WANTED]).

     Here is how you get these files using ftp:

     ftp  FTP.UTOLEDO.EDU (or 131.183.1.1)
     login: anonymous password: your_e-mail_address (please !).
     ftp> cd [.MMOD] for mail archives or cd [.CHEMISTRY....]
     for all other files.
     ftp> ls  -l (to  get the  listing of  all  files  which  are
     there).
     ftp> get file_you_want.
     ftp> quit.

     Of course, you need to know ftp to use it efficiently.  Here
     are some more popular commands:

     ascii - set text mode (for text files)
     binary - set binary mode (for binary files) - very important
     if you  retrieve compressed (*.Z) or tar files.
     cd -  change directory  (note that  since this  is a VAX VMS
     machine,  the   syntax  for   changing  directories   is  cd
     [.DIRECTORY_NAME].  [-] moves you up one level.
     get -  get a  file from  the remote  machine to your current
     directory  (no * allowed in the name)
     glob -  toggle (glob/noglob)  the use  of wildcard character
     (*) in filenames
     help - list commands of ftp
     ls - list files in the current directory
     mget - get all files fitting the pattern (* allowed)
     prompt -  toggle asking  (yes/no)  for  each  file  in  mget
     command
     pwd - tells in which diretory you are now
     quit, bye - end the ftp session

VII. DISCLAIMERS.

     The  list   is  organized   as  a   service  to   MacroModel
researchers.   By posting to this list you agree that the content
