Huddle meets all of your "must-have" requirements --

Huddle provides three different user interfaces.  Two are command-based, with
an intuitive command set based on VMS mail (of those two, one is screen
oriented, and one does not require a smart terminal at all).  The third
interface is a panel-oriented, user-extendable menu a-la Lotus 1-2-3 or
MS-word.  I don't expect that the VAXnotes keypad interface qualifies as a
menuing interface.  Since the user interface issues are the primary reason
Huddle beats VAXnotes in competitive evaluations, I expect that VAXnotes does
not meet your simplicity requirement, or at least, not as well as Huddle
would.

For multiple layer cabinet/drawer/folder/file structure, we add an important
hierarchical level to the standard conference/topic/reply structure:  the
ability for a conference to support any number of subconferences where the
aggregate structure can be managed as a single unit.  I'm not sure VAXnotes'
conference/topic/reply alone can meet your requirement.

We approach message archival differently than VAXnotes does.  VAXnotes
messages are permanent and must be explicitly deleted.  On the other hand,
Huddle allows you to set a message retention period, which can vary from one
day to permanent on a conference-by-conference basis.  System maintenance is
highly automated and does not require a great deal of human intervention once
the initial settings are chosen.

Huddle offers VT330 support, and support for any terminal whatsoever.  The last
time I checked, I believe VAXnotes required a VT100 or better, so if you have
non-DEC terminals or VTs older than a VT100, you're out of luck with VAXnotes.

Huddle meets all but one of your "like-to-haves".  Huddle does not have a
spell checker built in, primarily because we have viewed this as the
responsibility of the selected text editor, and your editor of choice should
provide a spelling checker.  We support MAIL$EDIT exactly as MAIL does, which
allows you select any editor, including third-party word processors.


Huddle's access controls are considerably more intuitive than the VAXnotes
member model.  Huddle's model is similar to the familiar VMS
authorization/privilege mechanism.  System-wide and conference-specific access
controls are provided with well-chosen granularity.  For example, several
system administrators may be designated, and a conference can have any number
of moderators under our model, all based on Huddle privileges they have been
granted.

Documentation-wise Huddle's entire user documentation can be found in online
help, and is provided in Bookreader format.  Our documentation is also
regarded by our customers as top of the line, including a thorough discussion
of the conferencing paradigm, how Huddle fits that paradigm, and of course,
both general and detailed use instructions and reference.

The following are features specific to Huddle:

- File import is provided from local VMS files and from remote PC/Mac files.

- A system-news/notice facility is provided for system managers as an
  externally-accessible conference (called NEWS).  This is designed to supplant
  the "message of the day" with a message system where users generally have to
  read login messages only once.

- Two-way mailing list integration is provided, so you could, for example,
  have Huddle act as a full bi-directional peer to INFO-VAX and other mailing
  lists.

- A non-text file transfer area is also provided.

- The ability to scan conferences for new messages and report briefly on their
  contents (like VMS MAIL does at login) is provided.

Stylistically, Huddle, like VAXnotes, follows the electronic conferencing model
and terminology more closely than it follows the Bulletin Board model offered
BULLETIN and PC-based systems, and is oriented towards the professional
environment.  Unlike VAXnotes, we also offer a good deal of "crossover"
functionality from PC-Hobby-BBS land, such as file upload and download.

