In article <1992Aug6.181038.25252@sci.kun.nl> markov@cs.kun.nl (D-b Hendriks/M Overmeer) writes:
>Hi all,
> 	I currently am using my VAX/VMS account for sending and receiving
>PSI mail. In the short future I will be enabled to switch to an Ultrix
>account, and I was wondering if Ultrix has the capability to do PSI
>mail.
>I know this Ultrix machine will be reachable over an X.25 line, so 
>software for X.25 communication will be used. On VMS this is
>PSI, which includes the mail suport. Is this the same for Ultrix?
>
>Thanx in advance,
>
>Dino
>

For plugging a synchronous modem directly into an Ultrix box, we used
X.25 Native Mode and the Wide Area Network device drivers.  This also
required a DECStation 5000/125 since the 3100, 2100, and 5000/200
serial ports couldn't do it.  I think there is now a turbochannel
board.

We use X.25 Access to connect a DECStation to an X.25 line via a VAX 
running P.S.I.  That way the DECStation doesn't have to have a 
direct connection.  The info from DEC said you could do this with Phase IV
DECNet, but we had to upgrade to Phase V to get it to work.  BTW we
experienced differences in the way a Phase IV socket and a Phase V
socket generated SIGIO's.  This is only a problem if you've written
your own programs that use DECNet sockets.  It doesn't effect X.25.

If you do any of this, you will probably have to learn NCL.  This is the
NCP replacement for DECNet phase V.  I'd like to hear from you (or anyone
else for that matter) once you have to see if your experience with NCL
was the same as mine.

-- Les Walker


