In article <1992Aug5.013952.5955@decuac.dec.com> mjr@hussar.dco.dec.com (Marcus J. Ranum) writes:
>	One, we don't "play games" - we meet the terms of our
>licensing agreements with AT&T. Per user licensing is annoying
>to *everyone* but since we pay a royalty to AT&T per user, we
>need to track user licenses. Some other UNIX vendors made other
>arrangements with AT&T depending on who was sleeping with whom,
>and when, so not all vendors "impose" user licenses.

I appreciate that the n-user ULTRIX licensing is a result of the
agreement with AT&T. However reading the writeup about DEC OSF/1 in DEC's
"Digital Customer Update", it seems that a similar scheme applies to
OSF/1 1.0. Why? Since 1) the OSF is just a "technology provider" and
2) there should be no AT&T agreements that apply, what gives?
We had hopes that moving to OSF/1 would free up the n-user
licensing but that doesn't look to be the case anymore.
Who are DEC paying royalties to for OSF/1?

As an educational site with UNIX source licenses, we can get DEC
unlimited user ULTRIX licenses for $1 a time, by telling DEC the
serial numbers of the machines we want the licenses for. So the impact,
for us, of ULTRIX licensing is just that involved in paper shuffling,
writing letters to DEC, and making sure we know when licenses expire 
(expiration can be a problem when you have a large number of
workstations). Nevertheless, DEC's image about user licensing does DEC
damage, especially with people who are used to Suns/SGI's.
(People who use SGI's tell me that there is no n-user licensing
with those machines. So does SGI have a deal with AT&T too?)

On a slight tangent, the current implementation of ULTRIX lmf
with its local machine database is also looking restrictive these
days. How is this meant to be workable when one has whole labs full
of machines? Running diskless/diskfull or even with DECathena?
There should be no reason why some software products (at least) can't
be run with a floating license scheme over the network.
Will this ever materialise for ULTRIX or OSF/1?


Phil Leverton
IT Services, University of Melbourne,
Parkville, Victoria, Australia.  p.leverton@its.unimelb.EDU.AU


