I always thought the 16-bit processor was for MS-DOS, and the 8-bit for CP/M.
Of course, the 8-bit processor handled the I/O for the 16-bit one when running
MS-DOS.  Anyway, you have to remember the micro market in the early 1980's.
Almost all the PD software was written for CP/M, and there was a large base.
It wasn't unreasonable at the time to offer a good CP/M and a lesser version
of that upstart MS-DOS.  Being able to run both CP/M and MS-DOS was a great
feature then.  But DEC was a *long* way from being able to market them well.
The thing I disliked about the Rainbow was that all the MS-DOS software had
to be written with it in mind... it wasn't PC compatible.
