I can't answer your first question in general, as only you can really determine
if you're getting what you pay for.  It is unlikely, though, that if you
discover a problem with VMS V5.2 that you'll be able to get a fix that
doesn't involve upgrading to a more current release, though it largely
depends on just what the problem is.

As for your second question, I am certainly not an authority on what the
particular "Terms and Conditions" were on your software licenses, but my
understanding is that when you purchase a license for a specific version,
you may run that version forever on the same system as originally licensed,
without paying additional fees.  Read your software license agreement to
be certain.

Perhaps you could enlist Digital's help in determining why your tools
"blew-up" when you upgraded your VMS release, and could then find a way to
fix them.  It is unusual for a non-privileged application, which does not
access VMS internal data structures, to break when the VMS version is changed
(I have programs linked on VMS in 1977 which still run today).  Feel free to
send me mail with a short summary of the symptoms and I may be able to
point you in the right direction.
