>If one goes out and buys, say, a DECstation 5000/20/25/33/100/125 or
>some such and runs ULTRIX on it (latest version), does DEC try to
>play games with restricting user-logons like they do on the VMS
>VAXstations?

	One, we don't "play games" - we meet the terms of our
licensing agreements with AT&T. Per user licensing is annoying
to *everyone* but since we pay a royalty to AT&T per user, we
need to track user licenses. Some other UNIX vendors made other
arrangements with AT&T depending on who was sleeping with whom,
and when, so not all vendors "impose" user licenses.

> E.g. is it okay to have a terminal server allow lots
>of telnet sessions into the ULTRIX workstation?

	Each logged-in user represents a "user" licensed except
for the console of a workstation. Some other vendors interpret
network logins as "not a real log in" since it doesn't come over
a tty and is therefore "virtual" and get away with it. :(  If
your terminal server has 10 users connected via telnet to an
ULTRIX workstation, you'll need to have enough user licenses
for 10 users. You can defeat licensing in a variety of ways,
such as replacing /bin/login with the version from BSD, or
by writing a multiplexing meta-server, but at that point you
are violating your license.

	And they say UNIX is an "open system". Urr, that should
be UNIX(tm).

mjr.


